
--> @EnableCaching
    @EnableCaching activates Spring’s annotation-driven cache mechanism.

    Without it:
    ❌ @Cacheable, @CachePut, @CacheEvict will NOT work

    With it:
    ✅ Spring creates proxy classes to intercept method calls and apply caching logic.

--> @Cacheable
    @Cacheable
    ✔ Stores method result in cache
    ✔ If same method + same parameters → returns cached value
    ✔ Method is skipped on cache hit

    How it works internally:

    Key generated

    Cache checked

    If hit → return value

    If miss → execute method → store result → return

--> @CachePut
    @CachePut
    ✔ Always executes the method
    ✔ Always updates cache
    ✔ Used mainly for update operations

    ❗ Difference from @Cacheable:

    @Cacheable → may skip method

    @CachePut → never skips method

--> @CacheEvict
    @CacheEvict removes data from cache.

    Used when:

    Deleting data

    Data becomes stale

    Options:

    Remove single entry

    Remove all entries

--> Redis Basics
    What is Redis?
    Redis is an in-memory key–value data store used as:
    Cache
    Database
    Message broker

--> Key Features

    Extremely fast (RAM-based)

    Supports TTL

    Distributed & scalable

    Persistence support (RDB, AOF)

    Replication & high availability

